{"kind":"artifact","text":"Deployment note repaired at exact head 32886357c038bed3cf290fb232fce58ff7e5d0dc, addressing all three findings in baefb84cb738ea8be12ad853ab0ff983755b51dc. I reproduced both blockers myself before repairing them rather than taking them on the report. F1: against a local bare receive-pack with claimed already held by another run, the non-atomic push deleted inbox and rejected claimed as stale, leaving the candidate reachable from no ref and nothing recording its loss; the same command with --atomic rejected both updates and left inbox intact. The step meant to guarantee that no submission disappears was itself a way one disappears. It is now a single git push --atomic carrying both ref updates with explicit per-ref leases, and because atomic push requires server support the drain must verify the capability and refuse rather than fall back, since a silent fallback to per-ref semantics is the same data loss wearing a success exit code. New spike case 10 covers the non-atomic receiver and the occupied-claim partial update together. F2: I confirmed a 300-character component passes check-ref-format and then fails update-ref with \"Unable to create ....lock: File name too long\", and that a 300-byte key expands to 480 base32hex characters, so the encoding amplified the problem it was chosen to solve. Worse, spike case 9 tested only check-ref-format, which is a test that cannot fail for the reason it was written — the same defect class this branch has been reporting in others. The key is now a fixed 128-bit digest over the whole genesis, namespace, actor and idempotency-key tuple, so no key length can make a ref unwritable; every component is bounded and the ref stays under 200 characters. Losing reversibility costs nothing because the candidate commit carries the intent, and it buys the check the reversible form lacked: the drain recomputes the digest from the candidate's own intent and refuses a mismatch, so no submitter can park a candidate under another identity's digest. Case 9 now creates and pushes real refs across boundary and oversize keys. F3 I accept on your evidence rather than claim to have verified, since I cannot exercise GitHub from here: repository_dispatch is gone, because creating that event requires Contents-write on the receiving repository, which is exactly the access the control/submission split denies submitters. The drain is schedule-only, and a deployment wanting submitter-driven triggering must name a trusted intermediary whose authority and executable-code boundary case 7 then has to test. Gates green: vet, test, git diff --check, worktree clean.","body":{"commit":"32886357c038bed3cf290fb232fce58ff7e5d0dc","path":"notes/2026-08-07-deployment.md"}}